# Image-owned defaults for one tide sweep.
#
# The entrypoint sources this file, then a workflow variable of the same name wins. Keeping the
# numbers here is what lets a change reach every consumer through `:latest` instead of a workflow
# edit in each repository; a workflow sets one only to override it.
#
# The three search knobs are thresholds, not opinions: a file over `TIDE_MAX_FILE_LINES` is a
# fact the detector reports, and the agent's job is the plan to split it. Raise one and fewer,
# larger smells are reported; lower one and the sweep turns into a style checker.
: "${TIDE_MAX_FILE_LINES:=800}"
# A test file is measured against its own limit: splitting a table of cases buys less than
# splitting a module with logic in it, so the same 900 lines is a different smell in `tests/`.
: "${TIDE_MAX_TEST_FILE_LINES:=1500}"
: "${TIDE_MAX_DIR_FILES:=30}"
: "${TIDE_MAX_FILE_IMPORTS:=40}"
# Debt markers, counted where they are written (a comment) rather than wherever the word appears.
: "${TIDE_MAX_TODOS:=10}"
: "${TIDE_MAX_TODO_AGE_DAYS:=365}"
# Commits the sweep fetches, which is what makes `todo_age` answerable: a one-commit clone blames
# every line to the head and would report 0 days forever. 200 covers weeks-to-months of a busy
# repository; the age of an older marker is capped at the window, which is the right direction —
# an age over the threshold is still an age over the threshold. Raise it for a slower, older view.
: "${TIDE_HISTORY_DEPTH:=200}"
# Model calls one sweep may make. Each candidate is one agent run that also tries the refactor,
# so this is the knob that bounds both the bill and the wall clock.
: "${TIDE_MAX_ITEMS:=3}"
: "${TIDE_MAX_TOOLS:=120}"
# Wall clock for one shell command, in seconds. pi gives a command no timeout of its own, so
# without this the only ceiling is the job's `timeout-minutes` — one `pytest` over a monorepo or
# one `pip install` on a slow mirror sits there until the job is killed, and a killed job files
# nothing from the candidates it already finished. The reviewer's 120 assumes a diff-sized
# command; a tide runs the project's own suite, so the floor is higher. Twenty minutes is longer
# than a real suite and short enough that a hang costs one scan instead of the sweep — a checked
# in command that legitimately takes longer than this belongs in a per-repository image.
: "${TIDE_BASH_TIMEOUT_S:=1200}"
# Issues a repository may receive in 24h from the tide, and the only thing that bounds a
# Thursday sweep from filing everything Monday's sweep already filed.
: "${TIDE_ISSUES_PER_DAY:=5}"
export TIDE_MAX_FILE_LINES TIDE_MAX_TEST_FILE_LINES TIDE_MAX_DIR_FILES TIDE_MAX_FILE_IMPORTS
export TIDE_MAX_TODOS TIDE_MAX_TODO_AGE_DAYS TIDE_HISTORY_DEPTH
export TIDE_MAX_ITEMS TIDE_MAX_TOOLS TIDE_ISSUES_PER_DAY TIDE_BASH_TIMEOUT_S
